WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:04.460
Spider-Man: Far From Home follows a literal
class trip, but it's also a head trip, and

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:06.940
that goes for the characters in the movie
as well as its events.

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:11.100
Some characters play bigger roles than expected,
some aren't who they appear to be, and still

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.520
others are a total surprise.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:16.600
Here are some characters from Spider-Man:
Far From Home with more meaning than you realized.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.900
Be warned: Spoilers are undoubtedly coming.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.660
As she does in Homecoming, Betty Brant first
appears in the Midtown High School news show,

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:25.720
and later she goes on the trip with the rest
of the class.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:29.780
Her role is largely comic relief, with her
sudden, hyper-close dating relationship with

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:31.420
Ned undercutting some tension.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:35.500
It's also her curiosity that leads Ned to
invent "Night Monkey," the great character

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:36.860
find of 2019.

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:40.320
What you might not know is that Betty has
as long a history in comics as just about

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:46.080
any Spider-Man supporting character, first
appearing in Amazing Spider-Man #4 in 1963.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.760
She and Peter met as employees of the tabloid
newspaper the Daily Bugle, where she was Publisher

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:55.520
J. Jonah Jameson's secretary, a role the character
also filled in the Sam Raimi-directed Spider-Man

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:56.440
films.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:00.920
Comics Betty even dated, then married Ned
Leeds, though the comics version of Ned doesn't

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:03.620
share too many similarities with the movie
incarnation.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:07.540
Comics Ned died, and now Betty is an investigative
reporter herself.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:12.380
Though Robert Downey Jr. doesn't have any
scenes in Far From Home, - well any new scenes,

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:16.700
he’s in the BARF flashback - Iron Man hovers
over everything like the Mark V armor.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:20.950
Obviously, there's his relationship with Peter,
which leads to Peter being entrusted with

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:25.880
"EDITH," the augmented reality system powered
by Tony Stark's glasses, whose initials stand

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:27.260
for "Even Dead, I'm the Hero."

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:29.260
There's also his connection to Mysterio.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:33.660
The big reveal that Mysterio is a fraud and
criminal is no surprise to comics fans, but

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:37.900
the detail that Quentin Beck once worked for
Stark Industries, creating the hologramatic

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:42.040
"therapy" tool we saw Tony use in Captain
America: Civil War, is totally new.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:44.120
"Plus, 611 million dollars

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.480
for my little therapeutic experiment?

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.920
No one in their right mind would have ever
funded it."

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:56.360
It immediately ties Mysterio into the MCU
in a compelling way, and gives him something

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:00.210
in common with The Vulture from Spider-Man:
Homecoming: hatred of Tony Stark.

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:04.070
It's ironic that Tony yelled at Peter for
making messes in Homecoming, but Peter's had

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:05.940
to clean up Tony's messes twice now.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:10.120
And Iron Man does technically appear at one
point, rising out of Tony's grave in one of

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:11.400
Mysterio's illusions.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:15.460
Based on the character design, it's a moment
that's a clear nod to fans of the Marvel Zombies

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:16.460
comic series.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:19.800
We've never seen Peter's Uncle Ben in the
Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.420
He's barely even been mentioned.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:24.960
Ben's violent death at the hands of a nameless
crook, thus teaching Peter the tough lesson

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:25.760
that…

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.060
"With great power comes great responsibility."

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:35.420
...is a story that's already been told twice
on film, in 2002's Spider-Man and again in

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:37.170
2012's The Amazing Spider-Man.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:40.920
Plus, Tony Stark has filled the role of Peter's
father figure in the MCU.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:45.460
But Ben's presence is felt in Peter's innate
humility and his deep-down need to help the

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:46.180
little guy.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:48.140
Peter carries his uncle's lessons with him.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:49.760
He also carries his suitcase.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:53.980
As Peter is packing up to head off to Venice,
there's a quick shot of a set of initials

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:56.930
Peter's luggage: "BFP" for "Benjamin Franklin
Parker."

00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:00.760
It's the most overt reference to Uncle Ben
in an MCU movie yet.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:06.140
Not only does blustery newsman and Daily Bugle
Publisher J. Jonah Jameson appear in Far From

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:10.041
Home's mid-credits scene after Spider-Man
gets framed for Mysterio's crimes, he's also

00:03:10.041 --> 00:03:14.400
played by the actor who played him in the
Sam Raimi Spider-Man trilogy, J.K. Simmons.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.180
"Spider-Man wasn't attacking the city.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:16.920
He was trying to save it.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:17.640
That's slander."

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:18.560
"It is not!

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:19.560
I resent that!

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:21.080
Slander is spoken.

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:22.600
In print, it's libel."

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:26.940
He looks a little different than he did in
the Raimi movies; Simmons' naturally bald

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:30.480
head has replaced the comics-accurate flat
top he sported in the earlier films.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.140
But he's perfectly Jonah in every other way,
stoking public sentiment against Spider-Man

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:37.150
and painting the superhero as a threat.

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:38.200
Or maybe a menace.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:42.340
Jameson's appearance and the news report that
accompanies it sets a whole new status quo

00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:46.820
for MCU Spider-Man going forward, in which
he'll surely be regarded with a lot more contempt

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:47.580
from the public.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:49.760
Can the Anti-Spider Squad be far behind?

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.360
Once Mysterio's plan involving the holograms
and drones is revealed, the audience is left

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:58.110
to wonder when the Nick Fury and Maria Hill
they've been seeing have been the real ones,

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:00.640
and when they've been fakes generated by Mysterio's
tech.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.260
Certainly it's worth parsing the movie to
see when the two former S.H.I.E.L.D. operatives

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:08.080
are holograms and when they're not, but even
the "real" Fury and Hill aren't who they seem

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:08.740
to be.

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:12.940
In the post-credits scene, it's revealed that
they've been Talos and Soren, the husband-and-wife

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:15.530
Skrull leaders from Captain Marvel, the entire
time.

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:19.519
Fury has asked them to take his and Hill's
place for a bit while they relax on a virtual

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:20.240
vacation.

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:24.420
That is, until Mysterio causes all that chaos
and Talos has to call Fury to come back.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:29.200
Knowing that recontextualizes every scene
with Fury and Hill, and invites a second viewing.

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:31.860
Also, Soren's a pretty great shot with a sniper
rifle, huh?

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:36.700
Though Mysterio builds them up as hyper-powerful
mythological beings, the Elementals are little

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:37.920
more than a trick of the light.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:42.400
They're realistic projections emanating from
the holographic technology Quentin Beck created

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:46.620
as a Stark Industries employee, and the damage
they do comes from the drones built by fellow

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:47.860
ex-employee William.

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:51.990
But, as Mysterio observes, people believe
they're real in a post-snap world, because

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:53.120
they'll believe anything.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:56.980
They're really close to being believable for
the comics fans in the movie audience, too,

00:04:56.990 --> 00:05:01.060
because three of them closely resemble the
Spider-Man villains Sandman, Hydro-Man and

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:01.820
Molten Man.

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:06.180
At one point, Flash Thompson even reads a
Wikipedia entry for Morris Bench, the comics

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:11.060
alter-ego of Hydro-Man, reciting a creation
story that pretty closely resembles Hydro-Man's

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:12.020
comics origin.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:15.560
It clearly seems like a fake-out directed
at all the Hydro-maniacs watching.

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:20.900
Brad Davis, who didn't get snapped and therefore
had five extra years to grow up and get handsome,

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:22.800
serves the role of Peter's romantic rival.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:26.740
Peter's so desperate to beat him out for MJ's
affections that he accidentally almost kills

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:27.720
him with a drone.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:33.000
There was a version of Brad who appeared in
exactly one comic issue: 1979's Amazing Spider-Man

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:34.520
#188.

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:38.440
Mary Jane Watson goes on a couple dates with
him while on a break from dating Peter.

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:42.590
He's an Empire State University football star
and kind of a jerk, but he doesn't take any

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:45.640
pictures of anyone with their pants down like
the movie version does.

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:50.380
Also worth noting is Jason Ionello, who appears
once again as Betty Brant's co-anchor on the

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:55.220
Midtown High news show, was a recurring character
in the comic series Untold Tales of Spider-Man.

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:59.990
He ran with Flash Thompson and a group of
other popular teens, often picking on poor,

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:01.060
nerdy Peter Parker.

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:05.140
Dimitri appears to be Quentin Beck's right-hand
man through much of the first half of the

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:09.780
movie, staying mostly silent while ferrying
Peter back to his Venice hotel and driving

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:12.280
the bus that takes the Midtown High students
to Prague.

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:14.840
We never quite figure out what his deal is
in Far From Home.

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:19.000
After the big reveal that Mysterio has a whole
team of former, embittered Stark Industries

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:22.920
employees working with him, Dimitri sort of
just disappears from the movie.

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:27.740
Pre-release rumors pegged the character as
Dimitri Smerdyakov, also known as The Chameleon,

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:29.400
one of Spider-Man's oldest villains.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:30.700
Maybe he still could be.

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:34.200
Someone had to hand that doctored video over
to the Daily Bugle, after all.

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:38.220
And, like the Skrulls, the Chameleon can change
his appearance to look like anyone.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:40.040
He could be hiding in plain sight.

